1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,291
A fabric needs a way in and out, and a clock. bob's pads double as test points, and its clock is not a clock at all, which is what makes it safe and checkable.

2
00:00:10,867 --> 00:00:15,868
Each of bob's pads is one block in VPR's eyes: an output pin and an input pin.

3
00:00:16,188 --> 00:00:25,734
In the hardware, each direction passes through a boundary-scan cell of the IEEE 1149.1 kind called BC underscore one.

4
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:28,328
Normally the cells are transparent.

5
00:00:28,648 --> 00:00:40,074
But they are also linked into one long shift register on JTAG, 88 cells for 44 pads, so the test host can read every pin, or drive every pin, in a single scan.

6
00:00:40,394 --> 00:00:45,110
While the startup signal GTS is high, the output cells force zero.

7
00:00:46,667 --> 00:00:49,839
Loading a design and watching LEDs proves very little.

8
00:00:50,159 --> 00:00:52,657
Boundary scan lets the tests do better.

9
00:00:52,977 --> 00:00:55,718
SAMPLE reads the real pins at one instant.

10
00:00:56,038 --> 00:01:01,927
INTEST lets JTAG drive the fabric's inputs, so a test needs nobody pressing buttons.

11
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:07,778
With autostep, each INTEST scan also advances the design by exactly one clock.

12
00:01:08,098 --> 00:01:13,825
And CAPTURE reads back every element's outputs, which for a registered element is its flip-flop.

13
00:01:14,145 --> 00:01:18,675
Together they let the board be compared with a golden model after every single clock.

14
00:01:20,233 --> 00:01:22,822
The guest's clock is not a separate clock.

15
00:01:23,142 --> 00:01:35,301
Every flip-flop in the fabric is clocked by the host's 125 megahertz system clock, and is allowed to change only on a cycle where a one-cycle enable, called gce, is high.

16
00:01:35,621 --> 00:01:38,247
One gce pulse is one user clock.

17
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:40,337
This buys three things.

18
00:01:40,657 --> 00:01:47,168
The timing tools see one real clock, which AMD's methodology guide recommends over derived clocks.

19
00:01:47,488 --> 00:01:50,945
The test host can step the design one clock at a time.

20
00:01:51,265 --> 00:01:57,212
And holding gce low freezes the entire fabric, which partial reconfiguration uses.

21
00:01:58,767 --> 00:02:02,395
gce comes from a small clock controller with two modes.

22
00:02:02,715 --> 00:02:07,697
Stepped by JTAG, it gives one pulse per test-clock edge or per step command.

23
00:02:08,017 --> 00:02:14,179
Free-running, it divides the system clock, and since milestone twenty any integer period is allowed.

24
00:02:14,633 --> 00:02:23,437
And in both modes it enforces a minimum gap between pulses: by default 512 system cycles, about four microseconds.

25
00:02:23,757 --> 00:02:26,082
A request that comes too soon waits.

26
00:02:26,402 --> 00:02:31,345
That guarantee is what makes the timing constraints true, as we will see in a moment.

27
00:02:32,900 --> 00:02:39,158
The JTAG side runs on the test clock, the fabric on the system clock, and the two are unrelated.

28
00:02:39,478 --> 00:02:51,239
So every control signal that crosses between them goes through two flip-flops marked as an asynchronous register, the textbook synchroniser, which gives a metastable first stage a full cycle to settle.

29
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:59,280
To rewrite part of a running design, bob must stop the fabric safely, across both clock domains.

30
00:02:59,600 --> 00:03:05,960
The host sends the AGHIGH command; the configuration logic raises a freeze request on the test clock.

31
00:03:06,433 --> 00:03:14,407
Two flip-flops carry it into the system clock domain, where the clock controller holds gce low and raises frozen on the same edge.

32
00:03:14,727 --> 00:03:20,194
Two more carry that back, and only then does the status register report GHIGH bar as zero.

33
00:03:20,667 --> 00:03:28,762
Now no register, block RAM or DSP register in the fabric can change, because every one of them is enabled by gce.

34
00:03:29,082 --> 00:03:36,579
Frames are written, and the LFRM command releases the clock, but only if a checksum written after the last frame matched.

35
00:03:36,899 --> 00:03:41,964
Otherwise the fabric stays frozen with an error, and a half-written design never runs.

36
00:03:43,533 --> 00:03:45,997
One more piece makes the clock trustworthy.

37
00:03:46,317 --> 00:03:52,670
Vivado times the fabric unconfigured, and unconfigured it is a mesh of loops through every multiplexer.

38
00:03:52,990 --> 00:03:59,751
At milestone twenty-one its worst path ran through more than six thousand logic levels that no design could ever use.

39
00:04:00,233 --> 00:04:02,393
So bob stopped asking Vivado.

40
00:04:02,713 --> 00:04:20,093
The constraints relax the fabric's paths, and the guarantee moved into software. timing dot py walks a configured design through exactly the inputs its bits select, and the contract refuses any build or load whose critical path, doubled, does not fit the spacing of its gce pulses.

41
00:04:20,567 --> 00:04:33,957
For the counter example, the critical path is 12.909 nanoseconds: through a crossbar mux, a LUT, the carry chain up through two blocks over the direct carry wire, and a flip-flop's setup time.

42
00:04:35,533 --> 00:04:46,897
The pads and boundary cells live in the top level; the clock controller is one file; and a dedicated testbench checks the spacing in both modes, early requests, the freeze and the release.

